---
import { Brain, Database, Lightbulb, OctagonAlert, Shapes, Sparkles, ThumbsDown, ThumbsUp, TriangleAlert } from "lucide-react";
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import EventExample from "../components/EventExample.astro";
import Equation from "../components/Equation.astro";
import { settings } from "../lib/data.js";
import { COMPLAINT_COLOR, PRAISE_COLOR, formatCount } from "../lib/games.js";
import { capitalize, themeIcon } from "../lib/themes.js";

// Share as a whole percent, for the numbers quoted below
const percent = (share) => `${Math.round(share * 100)}%`;
const s = settings;

// Page outline as (anchor, heading), matching the headings below
const dots = [
  ["why-steamtldr", "Why"],
  ["data-sources", "Data"],
  ["finding-themes", "Themes"],
  ["judging-tone", "Tone"],
  ["flagged-events", "Events"],
  ["written-summary", "Summary"],
  ["known-limits", "Limits"],
];
---

<Page title="FAQ" kicker="FAQ" dots={dots} lede="Where the numbers come from, how themes and tone are found, and what a summary can't tell you.">
  <Fragment slot="heading">How SteamTLDR <em class="accent-word">works</em></Fragment>

  <section class="panel" id="why-steamtldr" data-reveal>
    <h2><Lightbulb color={PRAISE_COLOR} />Why</h2>
    <p>
      Steam gives you one score, so finding out why players love or hate a game means reading dozens of reviews or watching a video,
      and a single number hides what they praise, what they complain about, and whether opinion is changing.
      SteamTLDR reads thousands of reviews per game and sums up the <em>why</em>: top themes, recent trends, and how new players compare with veterans.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="data-sources" data-reveal>
    <h2><Database color={PRAISE_COLOR} />Data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Reviews come from Steam's public review API, English only.</li>
      <li>Scores, monthly trends and the playtime split are <strong>Steam's exact totals</strong>, not estimates.</li>
      <li>Each game is a snapshot, and its page shows when it was last updated.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="eq-intro">The text sample per game, used for themes and tone (never for scores):</p>
    <Equation
      label="Sample"
      terms={[
        [formatCount(s.helpful_reviews), "most helpful reviews"],
        [formatCount(s.recent_reviews), `from the last ${s.recent_days} days`],
        [`up to ${formatCount(s.history_reviews)}`, "spread across earlier months"],
      ]}
      result={[`~${formatCount(s.helpful_reviews + s.recent_reviews + s.history_reviews)}`, "reviews per game"]}
    />
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="finding-themes" data-reveal>
    <h2><Shapes color={PRAISE_COLOR} />Themes</h2>
    <p>Each review is cut into clauses, and each clause is matched to a theme by its keywords.</p>
    <div class="clause-demo" aria-label="Example: one review split into two clauses">
      <div class="clause-review"><span class="cl-thumb up"><ThumbsUp />Thumbs up</span><q>Great story, <mark>but</mark> constant crashes.</q></div>
      <div class="clause-split">
        <div class="clause praise"><q>Great <b>story</b></q><span><ThumbsUp />Praise · Story &amp; characters</span></div>
        <div class="clause complaint"><q>constant <b>crashes</b></q><span><ThumbsDown />Complaint · Bugs &amp; crashes</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li>Clauses split at punctuation and words like <em>but</em> and <em>however</em>.</li>
      <li>A clause keeps its review's thumb unless the tone model strongly disagrees (see Tone).</li>
      <li>{capitalize(s.problem_themes.join(" and "))} are always complaints.</li>
      <li>Shares are weighted to match Steam's real score, so "23%" means about 23% of all the game's reviews.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="theme-matrix" role="list" aria-label="Themes and their keywords">
      {Object.entries(s.themes).map(([theme, words]) => {
        const ThemeIcon = themeIcon(theme);
        return (
          <div class="theme-cell" role="listitem">
            <ThemeIcon aria-hidden="true" />
            <div class="theme-cell-name">{capitalize(theme)}</div>
            <div class="theme-cell-words">{words.slice(0, 3).join(" · ")}</div>
            <div class="theme-cell-more">+{words.length - 3} keywords</div>
          </div>
        );
      })}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="judging-tone" data-reveal>
    <h2><Brain color={PRAISE_COLOR} />Tone</h2>
    <p>A simple model scores each clause from 0 (negative) to 1 (positive).<br />It learned from about 828,000 reviews from 200 games, using each review's thumb as the answer.</p>
    <Equation
      op="→"
      wide
      terms={[
        ["Words to numbers", "Count the words in a clause, rare words weigh more (TF-IDF)"],
        ["Learn the lean", "Find which words lean positive or negative (logistic regression)"],
        ["Score", "0 is clearly negative, 1 is clearly positive"],
      ]}
    />
    <p class="eq-intro">The score only overrules a review's thumb when it is confident:</p>
    <div class="tone-scale" aria-label="Score zones">
      <div class="tone-bar">
        <i class="zone low" style={`flex: ${s.flip_to_complaint}`}></i>
        <i class="zone mid" style={`flex: ${s.flip_to_praise - s.flip_to_complaint}`}></i>
        <i class="zone high" style={`flex: ${1 - s.flip_to_praise}`}></i>
      </div>
      <div class="tone-labels">
        <div style={`flex: ${s.flip_to_complaint}`}><b>0 to {s.flip_to_complaint}</b>Complaint, even in a thumbs-up review</div>
        <div style={`flex: ${s.flip_to_praise - s.flip_to_complaint}`}><b>{s.flip_to_complaint} to {s.flip_to_praise}</b>Keeps its review's thumb</div>
        <div style={`flex: ${1 - s.flip_to_praise}`}><b>{s.flip_to_praise} to 1</b>Praise, even in a thumbs-down review</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="eq-intro">Tested on 3 games it never saw (Helldivers 2, Cities: Skylines II, Hollow Knight: Silksong):</p>
    <div class="stats tone-results">
      <div><div class="cell-label">Right overall</div><div class="cell-stat">92%</div></div>
      <div><div class="cell-label">Negative reviews caught</div><div class="cell-stat">93%</div></div>
      <div><div class="cell-label">Called negative and was</div><div class="cell-stat">89%</div></div>
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li>A bigger pretrained model (DistilBERT) did worse: gamer slang like "feet first into hell" read as negative to it.</li>
      <li>The model is only used for themes. Scores always come from Steam's totals.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="flagged-events" data-reveal>
    <h2><TriangleAlert color={COMPLAINT_COLOR} />Events</h2>
    <p>
      Every month, each game's reviews are compared with what's normal for it. "Usual" is the median of the previous {s.flag_lookback_months} months, so a game needs at least 3 months of history before it can be flagged, and launch months never are.
    </p>
    <div class="event-examples">
      <div class="event-col">
        <Equation
          label="Negative surge"
          op="and"
          terms={[
            [`${s.surge_negative_ratio}×`, "usual negative reviews, at least"],
            [`${s.surge_extra_negatives}+`, "more negative reviews than usual"],
            [`${s.surge_score_drop} pts`, "below the usual score, at least"],
          ]}
        />
        <EventExample appid={553850} label="2024-05" kind="surge" title="Example: Helldivers 2, May 2024" />
      </div>
      <div class="event-col">
        <Equation
          label="Score drop"
          op="and"
          terms={[
            [`${formatCount(s.drop_min_reviews)}+`, "reviews that month"],
            [`${s.drop_score_points} pts`, "below the usual score, at least"],
          ]}
        />
        <EventExample appid={553850} label="2024-08" kind="drop" title="Example: Helldivers 2, Aug 2024" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="written-summary" data-reveal>
    <h2><Sparkles color={PRAISE_COLOR} />Summary</h2>
    <p>The TL;DR at the top of each game page is written by an AI model, then checked by code before it goes live.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The AI only sees that game's numbers and a few review quotes. Nothing from the web.</li>
      <li>Code checks every number and quote against those numbers. If anything doesn't match, the AI tries again, up to three times. If it still fails, the game gets no TL;DR.</li>
      <li>When a game's data is updated, its TL;DR is rewritten.</li>
      <li>The checks catch wrong numbers, not every wrong word, so trust the charts and numbers on the game page first.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="known-limits" data-reveal>
    <h2><OctagonAlert color={COMPLAINT_COLOR} />Limits</h2>
    <p>A summary is a good first read, not the final word. These are the places it can mislead, with an example of each.</p>
    <div class="limits">
      <div class="limit">
        <h3>Keywords can't read context</h3>
        <p>A theme is matched by words, so the same word can mean something else in a game's own world.</p>
        <div class="misread"><q>Killing the bugs is the best part of this game.</q><span class="counted">Counted as: bugs &amp; crashes complaint</span><span class="meant">Meant: praise for the enemies</span></div>
        <div class="misread"><q>I made so much money selling crops by summer.</q><span class="counted">Counted as: price &amp; monetization</span><span class="meant">Meant: in-game gold</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <h3>Sarcasm and negation</h3>
        <p>The tone model reads words, not intent, so a joke or a double negative can flip its answer.</p>
        <div class="misread"><q>Oh great, another crash. Love that for me.</q><span class="counted">May be read as: praise</span><span class="meant">Meant: complaint</span></div>
        <div class="misread"><q>It's not bad at all.</q><span class="counted">May be read as: complaint</span><span class="meant">Meant: praise</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <h3>The text sample isn't random</h3>
        <p>Steam returns the most helpful reviews first, which skews the sample, so every score comes from Steam's exact totals, never from the sample.</p>
        <div class="sample-bars" aria-label="EA SPORTS FC 27: about 25% positive in the text sample against about 50% on Steam">
          <div><span>Text sample</span><i style="width: 25%"></i><b>~25%</b></div>
          <div><span>Steam's total</span><i style="width: 50%"></i><b>50%</b></div>
        </div>
        <p class="caption">EA SPORTS FC 27: share of positive reviews.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <h3>A flag shows what, not why</h3>
        <p>An event says something happened that month, not the reason or whether the anger was fair.</p>
        <div class="misread"><q>Helldivers 2, May 2024: 5.4× the usual negative reviews.</q><span class="counted">The data says: a surge</span><span class="meant">The cause (PSN account linking) you have to look up</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  /* FAQ text runs the full section width */
  .panel.panel > p, .panel.panel > ul, .panel.panel > ol { max-width: none; }
</style>
